  {
    "job_id": "ashby:aef8f0e7-37cd-4d6e-96c9-3af72f588bae",
    "title": "Software Engineer, RL Data",
    "company": "Cursor (Anysphere)",
    "location": "San Francisco",
    "country": "US",
    "remote": false,
    "ats": "ashby",
    "source": "company-career-page",
    "posted_at": "2026-10-12T20:53:44.567000+00:00",
    "discovered_at": "2026-10-12T22:47:35.415943+00:00",
    "description": "Our mission is to automate coding. The first step in our journey is to build the best tool for professional programmers, using a combination of inventive research, design, and engineering. Our organization is very flat, and our team is small and talent dense. We particularly like people who are truth-seeking, passionate, and creative. We enjoy spirited debate, crazy ideas, and shipping code. Software Engineer, RL Data About the Role The data behind a model shapes it more than almost anything else. On the RL Data team at SpaceXAI, you decide what that data is for our agents: which problems they practice, what world they practice in, and how they're judged. You'll take a weakness, like an agent that gives up on hard bugs or misuses a calendar API, and turn it into training data that fixes it, then show the next model is measurably better. You form hypotheses about why data is helping or misleading the model, build the systems to test them, run ablations, and read the rollouts to find the bug, shortcut or mismatch behind a result. Every flaw you catch, or miss, shows up in what the next Grok models learn. What you\u2019ll work on Turning a capability gap into thousands of verifiable tasks \u2014 for example, planting subtle bugs deep in a real project's dependencies for the agent to root-cause. Building environments that behave like production: real codebases with their real dependencies, and simulations of apps like email, calendar, and docs that match the real ones down to search, errors, and pagination. Designing graders for open-ended work that separate good solutions from bad without penalizing valid alternatives or rewarding shortcuts. Building automated checks that catch reward hacking, leaked answers, broken environments, and paths the real product wouldn't allow, before da\u2026",
    "salary_min": null,
